The (open) neighborhood N(v) of a vertex v is the set of vertices \nadjacent to v, i.e. N(v) = {u | uv ∈E}. The vertices in N(v) are the neighbors of v. The closed neighborhood of a vertex v\nis the set defined by N[v] := N(v) ∪{v}. The degree d(v) of v is defined as the number of neighbors of v. The vertex v is \nisolated if d(v) = 0, and full if every other vertex is a neighbor of v, i.e. d(v) = |V| −1. A graph is isolate-free if there are \nno isolated vertices in it. The minimum degree and the maximum degree of G are denoted by δ(G) and ∆(G), respectively. \nA vertex u ∈V dominates v ∈V if uv ∈E, or in other words if v ∈N(u). Then v is dominated by u. A vertex subset \nS ⊆V dominates T ⊆V if and only if T ⊆∪v∈S N(v). A vertex subset S ⊆V is a dominating set of G if each vertex in \nV \\ S is dominated by a vertex in S, or equivalently if V \\ S ⊆∪v∈S N(v). A dominating set that does not contain any \nother dominating set as a proper subset is a minimal dominating set. The domination number of G, denoted by γ (G), is the \nminimum cardinality of a dominating set of G.\nA set S ⊆V is a total dominating set of G if every vertex in V is dominated by a vertex from S, i.e. ∪v∈S N(v) = V. Note \nthat a graph G has a total dominating set if and only if it is isolate-free. A total dominating set is a minimal total dominating \nset if it does not contain any total dominating set as a proper subset. The total domination number of G, denoted by γt(G), \nis the minimum cardinality of a total dominating set of G. Since total domination is in the main focus of this paper, let \nus suppose that every graph is isolate-free in the sequel. For X ⊆V, let G −X denote the undirected graph that we get \nfrom G by deleting the vertices of X.\n∗Corresponding author at: Bolyai Institute, University of Szeged, Aradi vértanúk tere 1, 6720 Szeged, Hungary.\nE-mail address: blazsik@server.math.u-szeged.hu (Z.L. Blázsik).\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2025.07.021\n0166-218X/© 2025 Published 